# High Efficiency Planar p‐i‐n Perovskite Solar Cells Using Low‐Cost Fluorene‐Based Hole Transporting Material

## abstract
For commercial applications, it is a challenge to find suitable and low‐cost hole‐transporting material (HTM) in perovskite solar cells (PSCs), where high efficiency spiro‐OMeTAD and PTAA are expensive. A HTM based on 9,9‐dihexyl‐9H‐fluorene and N,N‐di‐p‐methylthiophenylamine (denoted as FMT) is designed and synthesized. High‐yield FMT with a linear structure is synthesized in two steps. The dopant‐free FMT‐based planar p‐i‐n perovskite solar cells (pp‐PSCs) exhibit a high power conversion efficiency (PCE) of 19.06%, which is among the highest PCEs reported for the pp‐PSCs based on organic HTM. For comparison, a PEDOT:PSS HTM‐based pp‐PSC is fabricated under the same conditions, and its PCE is found to be 13.9%.

For commercial applications, it is a challenge to find suitable and low-cost hole-transporting material (HTM) in perovskite solar cells (PSCs), where high efficiency spiro-OMeTAD and PTAA are expensive. A HTM based on 9,9-dihexyl-9H-fluorene and N,N-di-p-methylthiophenylamine (denoted as FMT) is designed and synthesized. High-yield FMT with a linear structure is synthesized in two steps. The dopant-free FMT-based planar p-i-n perovskite solar cells (pp-PSCs) exhibit a high power conversion efficiency (PCE) of $19.06\%$ , which is among the highest PCEs reported for the pp-PSCs based on organic HTM. For comparison, a PEDOT:PSS HTM-based pp-PSC is fabricated under the same conditions, and its PCE is found to be $13.9\%$ .
## 1. Introduction
In the past decade, perovskite solar cells (PSCs) have shown immense potential as the next generation commercial photovoltaics.[1–13] Due to their advantages of easy fabrication, cost-effectiveness, and suppressed hysteresis characteristics, planar p-i-n perovskite solar cells (pp-PSCs), reported by Guo et  al. in 2013, have drawn massive commercial and research interests.[2,8,12,14–22] For achieving this configuration, a hole-transporting layer (HTL) was located between the perovskite film and the transparent anode (such as indium-doped tin oxide, i.e., ITO), to reduce the energy barrier between upper and lower layers, and suppress the potential interfacial charge recombination.[17] Because of their material diversity and low-temperature solution processability, $p$ -type organic hole-transporting material (HTM) are lucrative candidates for HTL. Incidentally, commercial applications call for novel suitable and low-cost HTM.[23]
In spite of being reported as an HTM in the first pp-PSCs, and often used as a reference, poly(3,4-ethylenedioxythiophene):­ poly(styrenesulfonate) (known as PEDOT:PSS) is limited by its acidic and hygroscopic nature, and mismatching work functions $(-5.2$ to $-4.9\ \mathrm{eV})$ , with the highest occupied molecular orbital (HOMO) level $(-5.4~\mathrm{eV})$ of $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{NH}_{3}\mathrm{PbI}_{3}$ (denoted by $\mathsf{M A P b I}_{3}\mathsf{)}$ .[21,24] Several polymer HTM have since been exploited for higher power conversion efficiency (PCE) and a PCE of over $20\%$ was achieved by the application of expensive poly(bis(4- phenyl)(2,4,6-trimethylphenyl)amine) (PTAA) as HTM.[25–27]
In comparison with the polymer HTM, small molecules are usually easy to be synthesized and purified.[14–17,28–30] And some of the small molecules with acid-anchoring groups can form the self-assembled hole-transporting monolayers (SAM), which can be chemically attached to the FTO surface and are capable of transporting electronic holes efficiently. The PCEs of the SAMHTM-based pp-PSCs reported have been achieved over $17\%$ [31,32] Grisorio et  al. have reported that the 3D structure of SpiroOMeTAD is beneficial for achieving high PCE n-i-p PSCs and the tailored 2D structure of the HTM generates a more favorable perovskites-HTM interface in pp-PSCs, which indicats the design of the HTM structure is essential for high PCE pp-PSCs.[33] The linear, organic, small-molecule HTM, usually with a simple structure, reach a PCE as high as $18\%$ for pp-PSCs, which is still lower compared to its polymer counterpart, and the higher PCEs were usually obtained using dopants.[15] Fluorene is a popular construction unit for the interfacial charge-transporting materials in the PSCs.[34] In this study, a linear HTM with a 9,9-dihexyl-9H-fluorene core, and terminated with N,N-di-p-methylthiophenylamine (denoted as FMT), was designed and synthesized. Methylthiol groups in the para-position of the diphenylamine, rather than methoxy groups, are beneficial for lowering the highest occupied molecular orbit (HOMO) energy level of the corresponding HTM, which is related with a higher open circuit voltage $(V_{\mathrm{OC}})$ .[35] The synthesis was carried out in two steps with the commercial starting materials. The pp-PSC device with indium-doped tin oxide $(\mathrm{ITO})/$ $\mathrm{FMT/CH_{3}N H_{3}P b I_{3}}$ $(\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3})$ /PCBM/Al configuration was fabricated, with FMT or PEDOT:PSS as the HTM. It should be noted that the FMT HTL has no dopants. Under optimal conditions, a high PCE of $19.06\%$ was achieved for the FMT-based devices. This value is one of the highest efficiencies for the linear-organic-HTMbased pp-PSCs. On the other hand, the PEDOT:PSS HTM-based pp-PSCs had a PCE of only $13.9\%$ . The results indicated that FMT is a high performance HTM for pp-PSCs.
are shown in Figures S1–S3, Supporting Information. FMT was synthesized by coupling dibromo-9,9-dihexyl-9H-fluorene and N,N-di- $p$ -methylthiophenylamine through a Buchwald–Hartwig reaction with a yield of $64.6\%$ . All of the starting compounds were acquired commercially. The synthesis of $N,N.$ di- $p$ -methylthiophenylamine was reported in our previous work.[35] The cost of synthesizing FMT is roughly estimated according to the cost model described by Petrus et al. and Li et al.[28,36,37] The cost of FMT is approximately US\$41.8 per g, which is much cheaper than that of PTAA ( $\mathrm{US}\$423.3$ per g) and Spiro-OMeTAD (US\$217.5 per g).
