## 2.2. Properties of Intrinsic FMT
Figure  1A shows the UV–vis absorption spectra of FMT in the solution and film. The molar absorbance of FMT at peak position is approximately $4.57\times10^{4}\mathrm{mol}\mathrm{L}^{-1}\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ . The absorption of the film ranges from 300 to $420\mathrm{nm}$ , showing a $15\mathrm{nm}$ bathochromic shift compared with the absorption of the solution. The FMT film is nearly transparent to visible light after the $420\mathrm{-nm}$ wavelength, where the transmittance is almost $100\%$ (Figure  1B). The transmittance of the HTM is one of the key properties applied in the pp-PSCs. The absorption edge of FMT film is at ${\approx}420~\mathrm{nm}$ , corresponding to a band gap of $2.95\mathrm{eV.}$ The electronic energy levels of FMT were measured by electrochemical cyclic voltammetry (Figure  1C). The HOMO and the lowest unoccupied molecular orbital (LUMO) levels of FMT were calculated to be $-4.89$ and $-1.94\ \mathrm{eV},$ respectively, according the equation, $E_{\mathrm{HOMO}}=-\mathrm{e}$ $E_{\mathrm{\onset}}^{\mathrm{ox}}+4.71\}$ $(\mathrm{eV})$ , where $\boldsymbol{E}_{\mathrm{~\onset}}^{\mathrm{ox}}$ is the onset oxidation potential versus $\mathrm{\sfAg/Ag^{+}}$ and $\mathrm{LUMO}=\mathrm{HOMO}+E_{\mathrm{g}}^{\mathrm{\opt}}$ .[38] When compared with the valence band of $\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}$ $(-5.4\mathrm{eV})$ and the work function of ITO $(-4.6\ \mathrm{eV})$ , the energy level of FMT is suitable to be applied as an HTM in pp-PSCs.
FMT exhibit good thermal stability with decomposition temperature of $387^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , at $5\%$ weight loss (Figure 1D). Meanwhile, differential scanning calorimetry (DSC) thermogram was conducted to reveal the crystallinity of FMT. FMT exhibited a melting peak at approximately $138~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and no crystallization peak during the heating and cooling process, respectively, indicating that the FMT film has an amorphous structure (Figure S4, Supporting Information). A space-charge-limited current (SCLC) method was used to investigate the hole mobility of the FMT film, and $\mathrm{ITO/PEDOT{:}P S S/F M T/M o O_{3}/}$ Al was used as the hole-only device configuration. The results were plotted in Figure S5, Supporting Information, as (current density)0.5  versus voltage. The hole mobility of intrinsic FMT was calculated to be $2.28\times10^{-6}\ \mathrm{cm}^{2}\ \mathrm{V}^{-1}\ \mathrm{s}^{-1}$ , which is relatively lower comparing with high efficiency HTM reported. Because the average thickness of FMT layer is investigated to be approximately $10\ \mathrm{nm}$ , the negative influence of the intrinsic FMT low hole mobility on the performance of the pp-PSCs maybe faint.
## 2.3. Photovoltaic Properties
Based on the properties of FMT investigated above, pp-PSCs with the ITO/HTL/MAPbI3/PCBM/Al configuration were fabricated (Figure S6A, Supporting Information). As shown in Figure S6B, Supporting Information, all the layers in the crosssectional scanning electron microscope (SEM) image of a FMTbased device can be clearly distinguished. The energy diagram for each layers is shown in Figure 2A. First, the impact of FMT HTM concentration on the performance of the pp-PSCs device was investigated. The current density–voltage $\left(J-V\right)$ curves of the photovoltaic devices prepared with different FMT concentrations are illustrated in Figure  2B, and the corresponding photovoltaic parameters are summarized in Table  1. The optimal concentration according to the efficiency was found to be $12.5\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ , and a PCE of $5.97\%$ was achieved with $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ of $0.83\mathrm{V},$ a shout circuit current $(J_{\mathrm{SC}})$ of $11.75\mathrm{\mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , and a fill factor $(F F)$ of $61\%$ (Figure 2B). After optimization, the PCE of the device can increase to $15.15\%$ with a $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ of $1.05\mathrm{V},$ , a $J_{\mathrm{SC}}$ of $19.83\mathrm{\mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , and an $F F$ of $72.76\%$ .
There is an interesting phenomenon that the PCE of the devices will go up continuously along with the storage time (Figure 3A). When the devices based on FMT were tested after $24\mathrm{~h~}$ , the PCE can reach $19.06\%$ with the corresponding $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ of 1.07  V, $J_{\mathrm{SC}}$ of $22.52\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ , and $F F$ of $79.27\%$ . External quantum efficiency (EQE) spectra of the corresponding pp-PSCs are shown in Figure  3B. The $J_{\mathrm{SC}}$ value calculated from the integration of the EQE spectrum was $21.19\mathrm{mAcm}^{-2}$ , which agrees with the value obtained from the current density-voltage curve, within $5\%$ deviation. The other three parallel FMT-based pp-PSCs devices were fabricated under the same condition and investigated after 0, 16, 24, and $40\mathrm{~h~}$ , respectively. The $V_{\mathrm{OC}},J_{\mathrm{SC}}$ , $F F$ , and PCE of the devices were plotted in Figure 3C,D. All the devices exhibit the increasing PCE with the storage time at first $24\mathrm{~h~}$ and their increasing PCE benefit from the incremental $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ and FF. A plausible reason for the surge in PCE with the storage time may be the thin FMT film (about $10\ \mathrm{nm}$ ), which is beneficial for the formation of better perovskites/HTM interface.[33] The X-ray diffraction (XRD) pattern of the FMT film on ITO as cast and after $24\mathrm{~h~}$ , the ${\mathrm{MAPdI}}_{3}$ film on top of FMT as fabricated and after $24\mathrm{~h~}$ , was investigated, respectively (Figure S7, Supporting Information). No change in the XRD pattern between the FMT film when cast and after $24\mathrm{~h~}$ was observed. For the $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ perovskites film on top of the FMT layer, the characteristic diffraction peaks at the $2\theta$ values of $13.98^{\circ}$ and $28.32^{\circ}$ increase slightly after $24\mathrm{~h~}$ , which indicates the perovskite film had crystalized better on the surface of the FMT layer after $24\mathrm{~h~}$ . The SEM imagines of the $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ and the $\mathrm{FMT-MAPbI}_{3}$ surface as cast and stored after $24\mathrm{~h~}$ have been investigated, respectively (Figure  4A–D). Both the $\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}$ surfaces as cast and storaged after $24\mathrm{~h~}$ exhibit defects, even more after $24\mathrm{~h~}$ , which will be traps and negatively affect the efficiency(Figure 4A,C). However the defects disappeared with time until compact and homogeneous perovskite morpho­ logy formed when $\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}$ film grow on the surface of FMT (Figure 4B,D), which illustrate the FMT layer is benefit for the homogeneous perovskite film forming and resulting in the growing PCE.
Device reproducibility was recorded by 54 cells using FMT as the HTM. The efficiency histogram (Figure  5A) indicated over $83\%$ of all the devices had a PCE higher than $17\%$ , and the device revealed a small hysteresis upon forward and reverse scanning (Figure  5B; Figure S8, Supporting Information). The storage stability of FMT HTL-based pp-PSCs at room temperature (RT) and at $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in a nitrogen atmosphere was investigated, respectively. During the $270\ \mathrm{h}$ , the PCE of FMTbased devices at RT had risen at the first $24\mathrm{h}$ and decline after $50\mathrm{~h~}$ , while the corresponding device had retained over $86\%$ of its initial PCE in the final hours (Figure 5C). At $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ the FMTbased devices had retained about $59\%$ of its initial PCE in $132\mathrm{h}$ (Figure S9, Supporting Information).
## 2.4. Steady-State and Time-Resolved Photoluminescence (PL)
The pp-PSCs with PEDOT: PSS as HTM was fabricated under the same conditions as reference. The $J{-}V$ curve of this photo­ voltaic device is shown in Figure S10, Supporting Information; the PCE is $13.9\%$ , with a $J_{\mathrm{SC}}$ of $20.1\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ , a $V_{\mathrm{OC}}$ of $0.92~\mathrm{V},$ , and an $F F$ of $75.5\%$ . To further understand the charge dynamics of the pp-PSCs with FMT as HTM, the steady-state PL was recorded. To make a parallel comparison, the samples were fabricated with the same batch of materials under the same conditions. The steady-state PL of $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ , $\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}/$ PEDOT:PSS and $\mathrm{MAPbI_{3}/F M T}$ are shown in Figure  5D. The $\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}$ film gives an emission band peaked at approximately $785~\mathrm{nm}$ . A distinct PL quenching was observed when the HTM layers for both FMT and PEDOT:PSS were introduced, which indicated an efficient charge transfer had occurred at the perovskite-HTM interface. Compared to PEDOT:PSS $(\approx73\%)$ , the FMT-coated perovskite film exhibited a better PL quenching efficiency $(\approx92\%)$ , indicating that the FMT layer presented an excellent extraction ability of charge carriers to PEDOT:PSS. The PL quenching studies provided a reasonable explanation for the $\mathrm{\high{\itJ_{SC}}}$ of FMT based pp-PSCs devices.
## 3. Conclusion
Through the Buchwald–Hartwig reaction with a yield of $64.6\%$ , a linear molecular HTM with 9,9-dihexyl-9H-fluorene as the center unit and N,N-di-p-methylthiophenylamine as the terminal (denoted as FMT), was synthesized straightforwardly from commercial monomers. The synthesis cost is calculated to be ${\approx}\mathrm{US}\$41.8$ per g. The FMT film was nearly transparent to the visible light. FMT possess good thermal stability and a suitable energy level for application in pp-PSCs. The pp-PSCs device with a simple dopant-free FMT HTM-based ITO/HTM/ $\mathrm{MAPbI_{3}/P C B M/A l}$ structure showed a high PCE of $19.06\%$ . For comparison, a PEDOT:PSS-based pp-PSC was fabricated under the same conditions, and its PCE was found to be only $13.9\%$ . Hence, FMT is one of the simplest and cheapest HTM materials applying for pp-PSCs reported, which shows a PCE over $19\%$ without requiring any dopants. Moreover, the FMT-based pp-PSCs tested after $24\mathrm{h}$ exhibited an interesting phenomenon of surging PCE. Although the reason behind the increasing PCE of pp-PSCs still needs investigation, FMT is a potent candidate for commercial application as the HTL in pp-PSCs.
